---
import ServiceComparison from './ServiceComparison.astro';
import PlaybookTestComparison from './PlaybookTestComparison.astro';
import { projects, path } from '../data/profile';
const project = projects.find(project => project.slug === 'stateful-evaluation')!;
---
<div class="environment-study" data-study-export>
  <header class="study-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">CURRENT RESEARCH / AI-BUILT TEST ENVIRONMENTS</span>
    <h1>Test AI workflows.<br /><em>Before they reach real systems.</em></h1>
    <p class="study-lead">I’m developing AI agents that build disposable test environments for security playbooks: automated workflows that query services and take actions such as disabling an account.</p>
    <a class="text-link study-jump" href="#testing-gap">See why a test can miss a bug <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
  </header>

  <section class="study-section problem-section" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">THE PROBLEM</span><h2 id="problem-heading">Generating a workflow is only the beginning.</h2></div>
    <div class="project-prose">
      <p>A playbook can use valid commands and finish without errors while changing the wrong account, IP address, or record. Checking the file or a successful API reply alone does not establish that it did the right thing.</p>
      <p>Testing its behavior requires a known starting state, an environment that responds to its actions, and checks against the task’s requirements.</p>
      <p class="problem-question">Watch what happens when a workflow blocks the wrong IP.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="testing-gap-section" aria-label="An example of a missed playbook bug">
    <PlaybookTestComparison />
  </section>

  <section class="approach-section" id="concept-demo" aria-labelledby="approach-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">OUR APPROACH</span>
    <h2 id="approach-heading">Let an agent build<br /><em>a test version of the service.</em></h2>
    <p class="approach-intro">Running these tests against real services requires access and suitable test data. Separate deployments can involve virtual machines, configuration, and cleanup. Handwritten substitutes also take work to build and maintain.</p>
    <p class="approach-intro">Our agent reads the service connector’s code and documentation, then builds an environment that simulates its responses and data changes. The same playbook and connector can use it for testing, with editable starting data and a reset between runs.</p>
    <ServiceComparison />
  </section>

  <section class="value-section" aria-labelledby="value-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">WHY THIS MATTERS</span>
    <h2 id="value-heading">Make repeated testing easier to set up.</h2>
    <div class="value-grid">
      <div><h3>Less infrastructure</h3><p>The aim is to reduce the cost and effort of deploying a full external service for every test setup.</p></div>
      <div><h3>Disposable environments</h3><p>Use a generated environment for an experiment, discard it, and build another when requirements change.</p></div>
      <div><h3>A repeatable starting point</h3><p>Reset the test data so different workflows can be tried against the same initial conditions.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="study-section contribution-section" aria-labelledby="contribution-heading">
    <div><span class="eyebrow">MY WORK & CURRENT STATUS</span><h2 id="contribution-heading">Building the environment-generation process.</h2></div>
    <div class="project-prose">
      <p>I develop the agent process that analyzes connector actions, generates the service implementation and starting data, and reviews whether actions produce consistent state changes.</p>
      <p>If an action blocks an IP, a later query should reflect the change to that IP. Independent test requirements are still needed to judge whether the workflow chose the right target; generating the environment does not establish playbook correctness.</p>
      <p>This is ongoing research. Lower setup cost and faster rebuilding are design goals; comparative cost and provisioning-time results are not yet reported here.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <details class="technical-details">
    <summary>Technical details for research and engineering readers</summary>
    <div class="technical-content">
      <p>Pipeline1 is the internal name of this environment-generation workflow. The test path retains Splunk SOAR, the security automation platform, and its unmodified service connector.</p>
      <ul>{project.approach.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul>
      <p>The generated implementation supplies external-service behavior, with resettable state initialized from seed data. Builder and reviewer agents use behavioral acceptance conditions to guide implementation and repair.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p class="study-download"><a class="text-link" href={path('/pipeline1-concept.svg')} download>Download the overview diagram <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></p>
</div>
<!-- End of portable research story -->

<style>
  .environment-study{max-width:1100px;margin-inline:auto}
  .study-heading{padding:25px 0 60px;max-width:980px}
  .eyebrow{color:var(--accent);font-size:10px;margin-bottom:20px;letter-spacing:.12em}
  .study-heading h1{font-size:clamp(42px,4.6vw,66px);line-height:1.09;letter-spacing:-.055em;font-weight:600}
  .study-heading h1 em,h2 em{font-family:inherit;font-style:normal;font-weight:inherit;color:var(--accent)}
  .study-lead{font-size:19px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.85;margin-top:26px;max-width:780px}
  .study-jump{margin-top:26px;font-size:13px}
  .study-section{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1.4fr;gap:65px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-block:50px}
  h2{font-size:34px;line-height:1.2;letter-spacing:-.035em;font-weight:550;max-width:820px}
  .study-section h2{font-size:29px}
  .project-prose>p{font-size:15px;line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:18px}
  .project-prose>p:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .project-prose .problem-question{color:var(--text);font-weight:550;font-size:18px}
  .approach-section{border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:54px;scroll-margin-top:115px}
  .testing-gap-section{padding-bottom:54px}
  .approach-intro{font-size:16px;line-height:1.9;color:var(--muted);max-width:820px;margin-top:22px}
  h3{font-size:19px;letter-spacing:-.025em;line-height:1.4}
  .value-grid p{font-size:13px;line-height:1.85;color:var(--muted);margin-top:10px}
  .value-section{padding-block:55px}
  .value-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:30px;margin-top:30px}
  .value-grid>div{padding-left:17px;border-left:2px solid color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 40%,var(--line))}
  .contribution-section{padding-bottom:40px}
  .technical-details{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;background:var(--surface-soft)}
  summary{cursor:pointer;padding:21px 24px;font-size:14px;font-weight:550}
  summary::marker{color:var(--accent)}
  .technical-content{padding:0 26px 23px;color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;line-height:1.9;max-width:870px}
  .technical-content ul{padding-left:22px;margin-block:18px}.technical-content li{padding:3px 0}
  .study-download{margin-top:25px}.study-download a{font-size:12px}
  @media(max-width:800px){.study-section{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:24px;padding-block:38px}.study-heading{padding:10px 0 40px}.study-lead{font-size:17px}.value-grid{gap:22px}h2{font-size:31px}.study-section h2{font-size:29px}.approach-section{padding-top:40px}}
  @media(max-width:540px){.study-heading h1{font-size:39px}.eyebrow{font-size:9px;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px}.study-lead{font-size:15px;line-height:1.9}.study-section h2,h2{font-size:28px}.project-prose>p,.approach-intro{font-size:14px}.value-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:24px}.value-section{padding-block:40px}.value-grid p{margin-top:6px}.technical-content{font-size:13px;padding-inline:20px}summary{font-size:13px;padding:19px}.study-jump{font-size:12px}}
  @media print{.study-heading h1{font-size:32px}.study-lead{font-size:12px}.study-jump,.study-download{display:none}.study-section{padding-block:22px;gap:30px}.study-section h2,h2{font-size:22px}.project-prose>p,.approach-intro{font-size:11px}}
</style>
